  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through next Wednesday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Upper level ridging over the\nsouthwestern CONUS continues to keep warm dry conditions over\nNevada with high temperatures expected to stay several degrees\nabove normal today and tomorrow. \n\nFriday, a series of upper level troughs will move across the\nwestern coast with a small system pushing south ahead of a much\nlarger system to the northwest. The smaller system will push the\nupper ridge east and bring in southwesterly flow over Nevada as it\nmoves into California. Weather gradients will tighten over the\nsilver state which will bring in stronger winds Friday afternoon\nwith wind speeds up to 20-25 mph, gusts as high as 35-45 mph. By\nFriday evening, the second larger trough will push in moisture, \nincreasing chances for isolated showers and mountain snow across \nnorthern and central Nevada that is expected to last through \nSaturday morning. Greatest probabilities for snow will be in \ncentral Nevada with snow levels dropping between 6000-7000 feet \novernight, accumulating onto higher passes and summits along the \nUS-50 corridor. Here are probabilities of summits and passes \nreceiving one inch of snow or more in 24 hours:\n\nAustin Summit - 90%\nBob Scotts Summit - 85%\nGarden Pass - 85%\nPinto Summit - 20%\nConnors Pass - 45%\nMurry Summit - 80%\nRobinson Summit - 55%\nEmigrant Pass - 40%\n\nSaturday afternoon through Sunday, a brief lull in the weather\nwith calmer conditions and lighter winds expected with cooler\ntemperatures being near or a few degrees below normal prior to the\nnext storm system on Monday. A third upper level trough from the \nnorthwest will move into the western CONUS on Monday that will \nbring increasing chances for periods of precipitation and mountain\nsnow that is expected to last through Tuesday morning. Snow \nlevels expected to start around 6500-7000 feet and drop to as low \nas 4500-5000 feet overnight as much cooler temperatures move \nin late Monday. Highs on Tuesday expected to be several degrees \nbelow normal with some areas reaching up to 10 degrees below \nnormal.",
